[ Exercise ](https://www.healthline.com/nutrition/10-benefits-of-exercise) is
recommended for boosting endorphin levels and improving mood. Multiple studies
have found that physical activity has a positive effect on the brain, which
includes improved mood (  [ 22
](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5928534/) ,  [ 23
](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5633699/) ).

Research indicates that dopamine is released in large amounts in the morning
when it’s time to wake up and that levels naturally fall in the evening when
it’s time to sleep (  [ 25
](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41386-020-00903-5) , [ 26
](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0168010217300573?via%3Dihub)
).

L-tyrosine, or tyrosine, is an [ amino acid
](https://www.medicalnewstoday.com/articles/324229/) that is a fundamental
part of protein. According to a  [ 2019 study
](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6647184/) , tyrosine increases
the availability of dopamine and may improve cognitive ability.

A  [ 2021 systematic review
](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8301978/) suggests that [
physical activity ](https://www.medicalnewstoday.com/articles/153390) may help
increase dopamine levels and improve mental health, such as reducing [ anxiety
](https://www.medicalnewstoday.com/articles/323454) and [ depressive symptoms
](https://www.medicalnewstoday.com/articles/8933) .

* González-Arancibia C, et al. (2019). Do your gut microbes affect your brain dopamine?
[ https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00213-019-05265-5
](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00213-019-05265-5)

[ Seasonal affective disorder (SAD)
](https://www.medicalnewstoday.com/articles/10306) may occur in months or
climates lacking sunlight and  [ may link to
](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK568745/) changes in neurotransmitters
such as dopamine.

