The depiction of the sitter in three-quarter profile is similar to late 15th-
century works by [ Lorenzo di Credi ](/wiki/Lorenzo_di_Credi "Lorenzo di
Credi") and [ Agnolo di Domenico del Mazziere
](/wiki/Agnolo_di_Domenico_del_Mazziere "Agnolo di Domenico del Mazziere") .
[37]  [ Zöllner ](/wiki/Frank_Z%C3%B6llner "Frank Zöllner") notes that the
sitter's general position can be traced back to [ Flemish
](/wiki/Flemish_painting "Flemish painting") models and that "in particular
the vertical slices of columns at both sides of the panel had precedents in
Flemish portraiture."  [39]  Woods-Marsden cites [ Hans Memling
](/wiki/Hans_Memling "Hans Memling") 's [ portrait of Benedetto Portinari
](/wiki/Benedetto_Portinari_Triptych "Benedetto Portinari Triptych") (1487) or
Italian imitations such as [ Sebastiano Mainardi's pendant portraits
](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Davide_Ghirlandaio_Retrato_de_dama_de_perfil_Gem%C3%A4ldegalerie,_Berl%C3%ADn.jpg
"commons:File:Davide Ghirlandaio Retrato de dama de perfil Gemäldegalerie,
Berlín.jpg") for the use of a _[ loggia ](/wiki/Loggia "Loggia") _ , which has
the effect of mediating between the sitter and the distant landscape, a
feature missing from Leonardo's earlier portrait of _[ Ginevra de' Benci
](/wiki/Ginevra_de%27_Benci "Ginevra de' Benci") _ .  [40]

