####  Vestibular system (balance)  [  [ edit
](/w/index.php?title=Sense&action=edit&section=31 "Edit section: Vestibular
system \(balance\)") ]

Sensory organs are [ organs ](/wiki/Organ_\(anatomy\) "Organ \(anatomy\)")
that sense and [ transduce ](/wiki/Transduction_\(physiology\) "Transduction
\(physiology\)") stimuli. Humans have various sensory organs (i.e. eyes, ears,
skin, nose, and mouth) that correspond to a respective [ visual system
](/wiki/Visual_system "Visual system") (sense of vision), [ auditory system
](/wiki/Auditory_system "Auditory system") (sense of hearing), [ somatosensory
system ](/wiki/Somatosensory_system "Somatosensory system") (sense of touch),
[ olfactory system ](/wiki/Olfactory_system "Olfactory system") (sense of
smell), and [ gustatory system ](/wiki/Gustatory_system "Gustatory system")
(sense of taste). Those systems, in turn, contribute to [ vision
](/wiki/Visual_perception "Visual perception") , [ hearing ](/wiki/Hearing
"Hearing") , [ touch ](/wiki/Touch "Touch") , [ smell ](/wiki/Olfaction
"Olfaction") , and the ability to [ taste ](/wiki/Flavoring#taste "Flavoring")
.  [5]  [6]  Internal sensation, or interoception, detects stimuli from
internal organs and tissues. Many internal sensory and perceptual systems
exist in humans, including the [ vestibular system ](/wiki/Vestibular_system
"Vestibular system") (sense of balance) sensed by the [ inner ear
](/wiki/Inner_ear "Inner ear") and providing the perception of [ spatial
orientation ](/wiki/Equilibrioception "Equilibrioception") ; [ proprioception
](/wiki/Proprioception "Proprioception") (body position); and [ nociception
](/wiki/Nociception "Nociception") (pain). Further internal [ chemoreception
](/wiki/Chemoreceptor "Chemoreceptor") \- and [ osmoreception
](/wiki/Osmoreceptor "Osmoreceptor") -based sensory systems lead to various
perceptions, such as [ hunger ](/wiki/Hunger_\(physiology\) "Hunger
\(physiology\)") , [ thirst ](/wiki/Thirst "Thirst") , [ suffocation
](/wiki/Suffocation "Suffocation") , and [ nausea ](/wiki/Nausea "Nausea") ,
or different involuntary behaviors, such as [ vomiting ](/wiki/Vomiting
"Vomiting") .  [2]  [3]  [4]

